es to blanket  the pacific northwest  fire crews remain on alert all across  the region  as hot temperatures spark fires a  wildfire in california is no different  than a wildfire  in oregon washington or even canada  they're all the same they all burn the  same  rob malcolm global news sacramento  still ahead the deep sea solution to an  electric vehicle supply problem  and the risks that could surface  the heat waves and wildfires that have  dominated canada summer so far are clear  examples of the global climate crisis  a recent survey found that more than 90  percent of canadians agree  electrification is key to climate action  sales of electric vehicles have surged  in recent years but  finding the metals to make all those  batteries is a challenge in itself  chris galas looks into one possible  solution and the risks involved  deep beneath the ocean surface the  metals company is hoping to help meet  the demand  for electric vehicle batteries  they're after poly metallic nodules  millions of little potato-shaped rocks  lying on the seabed people describe them  as an electric  vehicle battery in a rock because  everything you need to build a battery  cathode  is contained in these nodules high  concentrations of cobalt  nickel copper and manganese the nodules  are found in the clarion clipperton zone  a vast expanse of the south pacific 4  000 meters below the surface there's  something that they start on  could be a shark tooth quite often  or a little piece of shell or something  and the metal that is in solution  in the sea water slowly accretes to the  nodule so  it grows like a pearl over time atom by  atom  the metals company and about a dozen  other international players including  china and russia  have permission from the united nations  international seabed authority to  research  the unique ecosystem down there  [Music]  and they're testing new technology to  suck up the sunken treasure  with the lowest possible impact but some  say the nodules should stay right where  they are  undisturbed given we're in such a grave  situation with the climate emergency  we need to be really careful about  disturbing any kind of processes  that are storing and scavenging carbon  and helping to reduce  that threat of climate change the metals  company argues the risk to the planet is  far  greater if this new source of battery  making material  is not brought to the surface the  environmental and human  costs of land-based mining are well  documented  child laborers have died mining cobalt  in the congo  and recycling alone won't meet demand we  just don't have enough  today that we need to populate the  system and then close the loop  i think probably within 40 or  50 years or so there'll be enough metal  then  that we can keep using it over and over  again  the metals company is planning to go  public later this summer to help fund  its continuing research  and if approved its deep sea mining  operation  chris galas global news  next the group of bc swimmers with a  passion beyond the pool  [Applause]  nothing quite like playing on home ice  in front of fired up fans last night  more than 3  000 supporters packed montreal's bell  center cheering on the canadiens in the  team's first  home game in the stanley cup finals  series since 1993.  despite the energy the habs went down  6-3 to tampa bay  montreal is now just one loss away from  being swept by the lightning  open water swimming is not for the faint  of heart  and that's what makes it so empowering  for the spirit orcas  the victoria bc swimming group is  training to take on some major  challenges  but as jay durant explains these  athletes have  more in common than nerves of steel and  a passion for the pacific  this is the worst part what's the worst  part  maybe it's the cold 10 degree water or  perhaps the strong currents  or it could be the seaweed obstacle  course  none of this has slowed down the spirit  orcas so it's all about  myth busting susan simmons challe